      },
      "rev_n_1_9e9111_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_9e9111_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_9e9111",
        "thought_text": "Check if there exists any multiple of A that is congruent to C modulo B by iterating through multiples of A until finding a match or exceeding B. If found, return \"YES\"; otherwise, return \"NO\".",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_9e9111_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_bc9f0115d0",
        "created_at": 1794500818.708909
      },
      "rev_n_2_2f0acf_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_2f0acf_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_2f0acf",
        "thought_text": "Thought-1 (revised): Recognize that since we can choose any number of multiples of A, we should consider the properties of modular arithmetic. Specifically, if there exists any multiple of A that is congruent to C modulo B, then adding any number of such multiples will still keep the sum congruent to C modulo B. Therefore, we only need to check if C itself is a valid multiple of B when reduced modulo A. If C % A == 0, then it's possible to achieve the goal by selecting enough multiples of A. Otherwise, it's not possible.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_2f0acf_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_fd4cb76fcd",
        "created_at": 1794500830.3352897
      },
      "rev_n_3_28e2a4_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_28e2a4_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_28e2a4",
        "thought_text": "To determine if it's possible to achieve the desired sum, we need to check if there exists a set of multiples of \\( A \\) whose sum is congruent to \\( C \\) modulo \\( B \\). Simply checking if \\( C \\) is divisible by \\( A \\) is insufficient because it doesn't account for the requirement that the sum must be congruent to \\( C \\) modulo \\( B \\).\n\nInstead, we should use modular arithmetic properties to determine if there exists a non-negative integer \\( k \\) such that \\( kA \\equiv C \\pmod{B} \\). This can be done using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\( A \\) and \\( B \\), and then checking if \\( C \\) is a valid residue modulo the GCD. If the GCD divides \\( C \\), then it is possible to achieve the desired sum.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_28e2a4_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_56c8f18153",
        "created_at": 1794500846.4625647
      },
      "rev_n_4_2e6418_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_2e6418_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_2e6418",
        "thought_text": "Use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the GCD of A and B, and check if C is a valid residue modulo the GCD.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "390411714051203541",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794500855.4225006
      },
